WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:07.370
There's no such thing as a perfect film. More
often than not, there's some small scene that

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:13.030
prevents a movie from achieving greatness...or,
for some movies, even goodness. For some films,

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:17.330
these scenes can be so bad that they actually
tarnish the rest of the project as a whole.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:20.840
Some of these movies could've even been saved if they'd left their lousy scenes on the cutting

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:25.610
room floor, while others were already a lost
cause. For them, these scenes are just the

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:28.130
awful icing on the crappy cake.

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:29.440
SPIDER-MAN 3

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:34.030
To say that Peter Parker's dance scene ruined
Spider-Man 3 is a bit of an exaggeration,

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:39.340
since there's so much more wrong with the
film. The misfired Sandman CGI, the underwhelming

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:45.030
Venom, and James Franco's pie-eating performance
all contributed to the flop that was Spider-Man

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:49.809
3. But all of that pales in comparison to
Peter Parker dancing at the jazz club. Tobey

00:00:49.809 --> 00:00:54.320
Maguire's awkward hip-thrusting and finger-snapping
as he gazes through his messy bangs play more

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.800
like a bad SNL sketch than anything that ever
should have made it into a superhero movie.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.780
"Now dig on this."

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:12.020
How many people chunked in their popcorn when he actually said that? Because seriously, that's awful.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:13.630
THE MATRIX RELOADED

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:17.850
The scene where Neo fights a crapload of Agent
Smiths was supposed to be one of the major

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:22.790
action set pieces for The Matrix Reloaded.
But the second the fighting begins, both Neo

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:27.560
and Agent Smith get replaced with CGI so terrible
it makes The Sims look like virtual reality.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:31.619
The scene was meant to follow up the groundbreaking
"bullet-time" effects of the first movie.

00:01:31.619 --> 00:01:35.439
Instead, the botched effects proved to audiences
that neither Matrix sequel would even come

00:01:35.439 --> 00:01:38.020
close to touching the original.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:39.840
LIVE FREE OR DIE HARD

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.930
John McClane has done a lot of wildly unrealistic
things in his accidental encounters with terrorists.

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:49.159
But none have even come close to single-handedly
fighting—and beating—an actual fighter

00:01:49.159 --> 00:01:53.369
jet. Live Free or Die Hard is far from the
best movie in the series, but it was still

00:01:53.369 --> 00:01:58.200
pretty good even after being watered down
from an R-rating to a PG-13 rating. That is, until

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.530
the filmmakers just said, "eff it," and went
to crazytown in this scene. There's the laughable

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:06.420
ease with which a hacker takes command of
the Marine jet, to the Rube Goldberg-esque

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.630
way the highway perfectly falls apart to let
McClane escape, the entire scene should've just

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:15.070
stayed in the imagination of the six-year-old
who probably thought it up. Also, how is McClane able

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:18.830
to drive a semi? You know what? Never mind.
That's actually the least ridiculous thing

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:19.690
here.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:20.940
DIE ANOTHER DAY

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:24.690
With the help of a parachute, a piece of a
plane, and video effects that would embarrass

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:29.080
a high school A/V club, James Bond surfs on
what appears to be a hundred foot wave of

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:33.240
arctic water in Die Another Day. This scene has been cited as the death knell for

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:34.890
Pierce Brosnan's Bond.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.970
It took four years and the casting of Daniel

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:40.570
Craig for the series to redeem itself from
that time a James Bond movie looked like it

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:44.830
was ripped from a PlayStation 2 video game
cutscene. But we'll never forget, your Bond

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:48.290
died that day, Pierce. Make no mistake.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:51.980
STAR WARS: EPISODE 3 - REVENGE OF THE SITH

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:57.770
Of the many things that catch blame for "ruining"
the Star Wars prequels—Jar Jar Binks, midi-chlorians,

00:02:57.770 --> 00:03:00.710
almost every line of dialogue George Lucas
wrote for Padme and Anakin—

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.540
"I don't like sand"

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:09.340
—there is one moment that makes almost every fan cringe, no matter how dedicated. We're talking about

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:13.810
Anakin Skywalker's charred transformation
into Darth Vader, literally the jumping-off

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:15.910
of the entire Star Wars saga.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:19.950
In this moment, Vader learns he's lost his
wife and unborn children...and has been transformed

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:25.130
into, like, a Space Robocop. So, what does
he do? He does this:

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.320
"NNNOOOOOOOOOO"

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:44.720
It felt silly when it should have been tear-jerking,
and it capped a performance by Hayden Christensen

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:47.980
that was so unlikeable that he went from that
guy poised to be this generation's Harrison

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:52.420
Ford to the guy who was in that bank heist
movie with Chris Brown and T.I.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:54.940
JURASSIC PARK: THE LOST WORLD

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:58.220
If you ever found yourself being attacked
by a velociraptor, you'd probably wet your

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:02.200
pants and run away screaming. With that in
mind, it's impossible not to lose total faith

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:06.410
in Jurassic Park: The Lost World when Kelly
Malcolm, the spunky daughter of Dr. Ian Malcolm,

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:09.490
stands her ground against one of the lethal
dinosaurs by putting her gymnastic training

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:13.730
to the test. Watching a kid hold her own against
one of the franchise's nastiest villains is

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:18.830
undeniably cool, but the way she does it—hauling
off and kicking the creature through a window—is

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:23.020
entirely unbelievable. Sure, this is a movie
about about dinosaurs being brought back to

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:27.360
life. But dino-fighting-gymnastic skills are
just a bridge too far.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.240
LORD OF THE RINGS: THE RETURN OF THE KING

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:33.069
The Lord of the Rings trilogy's leisurely
pace is part of its charm. When you watch

00:04:33.069 --> 00:04:36.889
one of these movies, you know you're settling
in for a truly epic adventure. But even by

00:04:36.889 --> 00:04:40.400
those standards, the concluding chapter, The
Return of the King, goes on for what seems

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:45.650
like forever—especially in the final act,
where it seems to have, like, four or five endings.

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:49.240
It's always hard to say goodbye to a great
cinematic saga, so we can't begrudge director

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:53.840
Peter Jackson his fond Frodo farewell. Except
for the scene where the Hobbits frolic on

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:58.039
top of a bed in gauzy light and slow motion.
After spending all those hours with Frodo,

00:04:58.039 --> 00:05:03.060
Samwise, and the rest of the gang, we want
to be laughing with them. Not at them.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.490
TRANSFORMERS: REVENGE OF THE FALLEN

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:09.650
Most people have never gone to a Transformers
movie expecting a sensible plot or characters

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:14.080
worth caring that much about. We line up for these things for awesome special effects, and director

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:18.210
Michael Bay is more than happy to deliver.
Unfortunately, Bay isn't always content to

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:22.110
just blow stuff up. Sometimes he wants to
be funny, too. And that's what led to the

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:27.150
introduction of Skids and Mudflap in Revenge
of the Fallen. Forget about robots in disguise:

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:31.319
these two are racist caricatures out in the
open, trading on every offensive stereotype

00:05:31.319 --> 00:05:32.299
in the book.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:37.200
"Aw look at this motha...Mean robot sir!"

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:44.680
Bay later tried to throw the voice actors under the bus by blaming the characters' demeanor on faulty line readings, but no one bought it. Skids and Mudflap were

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:47.560
even cut from the next movie in the franchise,
Dark of the Moon.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:49.980
INDIANA JONES AND THE KINGDOM OF THE CRYSTAL
SKULL

00:05:50.169 --> 00:05:53.999
By the time Indiana Jones and the Kingdom
of the Crystal Skull arrived in 2008, we'd

00:05:53.999 --> 00:05:58.319
been waiting for a new Indy adventure for
nearly 20 years. Almost nothing could've killed

00:05:58.319 --> 00:06:01.800
our buzz for getting to spend another two
hours with our favorite archaeologist. In

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:05.930
the end, while it's no one's favorite Indiana
Jones movie, most folks were willing to forgive

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:11.810
most of its flaws—except the infamous
"nuking the fridge" sequence. Even in a movie

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:15.990
that asks us to suspend our disbelief far
enough to accept Indy making contact with

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:20.229
aliens, this awful action set piece—which
finds Indy miraculously surviving a nuclear

00:06:20.229 --> 00:06:25.099
test blast by using a lead-lined refrigerator
as shelter—is laughable, and ranks as one

00:06:25.099 --> 00:06:28.259
of the few true missteps in a beloved franchise.

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:29.540
MAN OF STEEL

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:33.729
The first four Superman movies weren't all
great films by any means, but they had a certain

00:06:33.729 --> 00:06:37.840
sweetness about them that reflected the character's
optimism in the comics. All that changed when

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:42.300
Zack Snyder took the reins for 2013's Man
of Steel. &nbsp;Although darker overtones were

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:46.310
probably to be expected in the post-Dark Knight
world, it was still difficult for many longtime

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:50.150
fans to accept the rampant, casual destruction
Superman helped unleash during his battle

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:54.759
with Zod in the final act. Even harder to
accept was his decision to end the fight...by

00:06:54.759 --> 00:06:59.249
snapping Zod's neck. Snyder has defended his
decision repeatedly, but that plot point remains

00:06:59.249 --> 00:07:04.270
the focus of intense debate. For some fans,
it undermines the entire concept of the character,

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:08.419
and when they saw it go down in the theater,
they had one unavoidable reaction:

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.940
"NNNOOOOOOOOOO"

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:14.020
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to see more videos like the one you

00:07:14.029 --> 00:07:17.309
just saw. And leave us a comment to let us
know what ridiculous movie scenes you think

00:07:17.309 --> 00:07:18.389
should've made the list.

